Intro

1:1 cooperating with (my twin brother) @LucDupAtStackExchange we managed to formally prove the conjecture stated in OP as bonus.

Since he (Luc) is stronger than me (Marc) in using Lean, he was better placed to implement the ideas that came up during past months.

**caveat**: both me and he have used AI to do some of the heavy lifting work. In fact this post, with exception of this Intro, was written by AI, and, I am affirmative to admit this: it is a well written summary of the solution. We did not keep track of the contributions of one another and AI. In fact, several contributions ware inspired by answers and comments here and in the in OP referenced related other stack exchange posts.

In appendix I will add formal the Lean proof and a somewhat compact Julia implementation which finds the solutions very fast up to huge numbers $n$. In fact, we did some benchmarks and the speed seems an order of magnitude $n$ faster than the mathoverflow#510135 answer code implementing the 'Le Maohua, Li Weixuan and Edward T.Wang' paper verbatim. This should perhaps not come as a surprise: all $(n,c)$-coins solutions for **all** $c$, given $n$, are incrementally encoded on **one** single $n \times n$ square board.

notes:

1. In this answer queens are called coins and a chessboard is called a (square) board.

2. Sometimes indices and values are $\{ 0, \dots, n-1 \}$ sometimes $\{ 1, \dots, n \}$.

---

The Incremental Board

Instead of constructing a separate configuration for each threshold $c$ (which leads to tedious, disjoint case-splits), we establish a stronger structural object: a single **Incremental Board** represented by an $n \times n$ incremental Latin square $L$. 

An $n \times n$ Latin square $L$ is **incremental** if:
1. Every row and column is a bijection onto $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$.
2. For every threshold $0 \le k < n$, every sum-diagonal and every difference-diagonal contains at most $k+1$ cells with value $L_{i,j} \le k$.

Given such a square, the subsets $S_c = \{(i,j) \mid L_{i,j} < c\}$ simultaneously solve the $(n,c)$-coins problem for every $0 \le c \le n$, encoded on a single board.

---

The Injective-Majorization Principle

Verifying diagonal capacity constraints for piecewise permutations directly via interval summation quickly blows up in complexity. We bypass this entirely using the **Injective-Majorization Principle**:

Lemma (Injective-Majorization Principle):

>! Let $D$ be any diagonal (sum or difference) on the board and let $v(i,j) = L_{i,j}$ be the cell value, **if** there exists a function $f(i,j) \le v(i,j)$ which is *injective* when restricted to the cells of $D$, **then** the number of cells on $D$ satisfying $v(i,j) \le k$ is at most $k+1$.

Proof Sketch: 

>! For every cell $(i,j) \in D$ where $v(i,j) \le k$, we have $0 \le f(i,j) \le v(i,j) \le k$. Because $f$ is injective along $D$, these values map one-to-one into the discrete range $\{0, 1, \dots, k\}$, which has size $k+1$. By the Pigeonhole Principle, the number of such cells cannot exceed $k+1$. $\blacksquare$

This reduces checking simultaneous diagonal inequalities to finding simple, well-behaved lower bounds that are injective along diagonals.

---

Constructing the Cyclic Latin Squares via Column Permutations

For $n = 5$ and all $n \ge 7$, the incremental board can be constructed compactly as a cyclic Latin square defined by:

>! $$L_{i,j} = (i + q(j)) \bmod n$$ with values mapped to $[ 1 \dots n ]$

The column permutation functions $q(j)$ (for $j \in 0 \dots n-1$) are partitioned into four infinite residue families modulo 6:

1. Family 1: $n \equiv 1, 5 \pmod 6 \quad (n \ge 5)$

>! Linear Knight-Slope Permutation:

>! $$q(j) = (-2j) \bmod n$$
>! No majorant shifts are needed ($f = v$), making this the cleanest family.

2. Family 2: $n \equiv 2, 4 \pmod 6 \quad (n \ge 10)$

>! 2-Block Step-2 Permutation with a Parity Jump:

>! Let $d = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$. We split the columns at the midpoint:

>! $$q(j) = \begin{cases} (2j + d + 1) \bmod n & \text{if } j < d \\ (2(j - d) + d + 4) \bmod n & \text{if } j \ge d \end{cases}$$

3. Family 3: $n \equiv 0 \pmod 6 \quad (n \ge 12)$

>! 5-Piece Step with Boundary Pins:

>! Because composites sharing factors with 2 and 3 create diagonal resonances, a m The columns are split into three structural thirds with step $+3$ and systematic block jumps:ore delicate 5-piece layout with boundary pins is required.

>! Let $d = \lfloor n/2 \rfloor$:

>! $$q(j) = \begin{cases} n - 2 & \text{if } j = 0 \\ 2j + 2 & \text{if } 0 < j < d - 2 \\ 0 & \text{if } j = d - 2 \\ 2(j - d + 1) + 1 & \text{if } d - 2 < j < n - 1 \\ 2 & \text{if } j = n - 1 \end{cases}$$

4. Family 4: $n \equiv 3 \pmod 6 \quad (n \ge 27)$

>! 3-Block Step-3 Permutation:

>! The columns are split into three structural thirds with step $+3$ and systematic block jumps:

>! Let $d = \lfloor n/3 \rfloor$.

>! $$q(j) = \begin{cases} (3j + 2d) \bmod n & \text{if } j < d \\ (3(j - d) + 2d + 4) \bmod n & \text{if } d \le j < 2d \\ (3(j - 2d) + 2d + 8) \bmod n & \text{if } j \ge 2d \end{cases}$$

---

Exceptional Small Cases

>! For small dimensions not covered by the asymptotic bounds above ($n \in \{4, 6, 8, 9, 15, 21\}$), explicit non-cyclic incremental squares handle the base cases.

>! For instance, at $n = 6$, the non-cyclic incremental square is given by:

>! $$L = \begin{pmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 & 4 & 6 & 5 \\4 & 2 & 5 & 1 & 3 & 6 \\5 &4 & 6 & 3 & 2 & 1 \\1 & 3 & 2 & 6 & 5 & 4 \\3 & 6 & 1 & 5 & 4 & 2 \\6 & 5 & 4 & 2 & 1 & 3\end{pmatrix}$$

----

Below find Julia code implementing and verifying Lean functionality to construct incremental boards and their nc coins solutions:


    # ============================================================================
    # Incremental (n,c)-Coins Generator, Verifier, and Display Engine
    #  twin Based
    # on Luc Duponcheel's Lean formalization
    #  and
    # on some Marc Duponcheel's ideas and reports
    # ============================================================================
    
    # ============================================================================
    # 1. Utilities
    # ============================================================================
    """
     * cyclic(n,q)
    Constructs the 1-indexed cyclic Latin square L[i,j] = 1 + ((i + q[j]) mod n).
    """
    cyclic(n,q) = [mod(i+q[j],n) + 1 for i in 0:(n-1), j in 1:n]
    
    # ============================================================================
    # 2. Families (compressed somewhat compact formulas extracted from Lean)
    # ============================================================================
    
    # Family 1: n = 1 or 5 (mod 6) (n >= 1)
    F_1(n,j) = j == 0 ? 0 : 2j < n ? n - 2j : 2n - 2j
    
    # Family 2: n = 2 or 4 (mod 6) (n >= 10)
    F_2(n,j) = (d = div(n,2); r = j < d ? 2j + d + 1 : 2(j - d) + d + 4; r < n ? r : r - n)
    
    # Family 3: n = 0 (mod 6) (n >= 12)
    F_3(n,j) = (d = div(n,2); j == 0 ? n - 2 : j < d - 2 ? 2j + 2 : j == d - 2 ? 0 : j < n - 1 ? 2(j - d + 1) + 1 : 2)
    
    # Family 4: n = 3 (mod 6) (n >= 27)
    F_4(n,j) = (d = div(n,3); r = j < d ? 3j + 2d : j < 2d ? 3(j - d) + 2d + 4 : 3(j - 2d) + 2d + 8; r < n ? r : r - n)
    
    # some finite small Permutations (0-indexed) below minimal values supported per family
    const Qs = Dict(
        8  => [0,4,1,3,6,2,7,5], # f2: 8 < 10
        9  => [0,6,3,1,8,5,2,4,7], # f3: 9 < 27
        15 => [0,3,6,9,12,8,5,2,13,1,4,7,10,14,11], # f3: 15 < 27
        21 => [0,18,15,12,9,6,3,7,4,1,14,17,10,16,2,5,11,8,20,13,19] # f3: 21 < 27
    )
    
    # get F_f where 1 <= f <= 4 providing q for Family f
    function get_f_q(n)
        if haskey(Qs,n)
            return (n,j) -> Qs[n][j+1]
        end
    
        # minimal values per family
        (m_2,m_3,m_4) = (10,12,27)
    
        r = mod(n,6)
        if r == 1 || r == 5
            return F_1
        elseif r == 2 || r == 4
            n >= m_2 || error("Family 2 requires n >= $m_2 (n = $n)")
            return F_2
        elseif r == 0
            n >= m_3 || error("Family 3 requires n >= $m_3 (n = $n)")
            return F_3
        elseif r == 3
            n >= m_4 || error("Family 4 requires n >= $m_4 (n = $n)")
            return F_4
        else
            error("Unsupported Dimension n = $n")
        end
    end
    
    # ============================================================================
    # 3. board Verification
    # ============================================================================
    """
     * correct(L,n)
    Verifies that L is a Latin square (bijective rows and columns) and that 
    for every threshold c (1 <= c <= n), every sum-diagonal and dif-diagonal 
    contains at most c cells with value <= c.
    """
    function correct(L,n)
        # 1. Check Row and Col Bijectivity
        for r in 1:n
            if !isperm(L[r,:])
                return false,"Row $r is not a Permutation"
            end
        end
        for c in 1:n
            if !isperm(L[:,c])
                return false,"Col $c is not a Permutation"
            end
        end
    
        # 2. Check Diagonal Capacities for every threshold c
        for c in 1:n
            # Check all sum-diagonals (0-indexed sum s from 0 to 2n-2)
            for s in 0:(2n-2)
                cnt = count(i->L[i+1,s-i+1] <= c, max(0,s-n+1):min(n-1,s))
                if cnt > c
                    return false,
                    "Sum-diagonal $s count $cnt > $c"
                end
            end
    
            # Check all dif-diagonals (0-indexed dif d from -(n-1) to n-1)
            for d in -(n-1):(n-1)
                cnt = count(i->L[i+1,i-d+1] <= c, max(0,d):min(n-1,n-1+d))
                if cnt > c
                    return false,
                    "Dif-diagonal $d count $cnt > $c"
                end
            end
        end
    
        return true,"verification success!"
    end
    
    # ============================================================================
    # 4. Solver & Display
    # ============================================================================
    """
     * solve(range;print_q,print_L,print_I,verify,benchmark)
    Generates, optionally benchmarks, verifies, displays any range of n and its c's.
    """
    function solve(range;print_q=true,print_L=true,print_I=false,verify=true,benchmark=false)
        if benchmark
            (e,t) = (2,time())
        end
    
        for n in range
            # pad length
            l = Int(floor(log(10,n)))+2
    
            if !benchmark
                println("Incremental board for n = $n")
            end
    
            if n < 4
                println("KO? Dimension n = $n < 4")
                continue
            elseif n == 4
                # an Explicit non-cyclic L4
                L = [
                    3 1 2 4;
                    2 4 3 1;
                    1 3 4 2;
                    4 2 1 3
                ]
                q = missing
            elseif n == 6
                # an Explicit non-cyclic L6
                L = [
                    2 1 3 4 6 5;
                    4 2 5 1 3 6;
                    5 4 6 3 2 1;
                    1 3 2 6 5 4;
                    3 6 1 5 4 2;
                    6 5 4 2 1 3
                ]
                q = missing
            else
                q = [get_f_q(n)(n,j) for j in 0:(n-1)]
                L = cyclic(n,q)
                q = q .+ 1
            end
    
            if verify
                ok,msg = correct(L,n)
                v = ok ? "OK!: SUCCESS" : "KO?: FAILURE"
                println("$v -> $msg")
            end
    
            if print_q
                println("column Permutation (1-indexed):")
                print("q = ")
                println(q)
            end
    
            if print_L
                println("Incremental Latin Square:")
                for r in eachrow(L)
                    println(" ", join(lpad.(r,l)))
                end
            end
    
            if print_I
                println("(n,c) Solutions ((0|1) valued):")
                for c in 1:n
                    println("Increment c = $c:")
                    for r in eachrow(L)
                        println(" ", join(lpad.(Int.(r.<=c),2),""))
                    end
                end
            end
    
            if benchmark
                if (n == 2^e)
                    s = (time() - t) / n
                    println("n = $n, t~/n = $s")
                    e += 1
                end
            else
                println()
            end
        end
    end
    
    # ============================================================================
    # Example Execution range r
    # ============================================================================
    r = collect(4:32)
    solve(r,print_q=true,print_L=true,print_I=true,verify=true)
    # r = collect(4:1024)
    # solve(r,print_q=false,print_L=false,print_I=false,verify=false,benchmark=false)
    
    exit(0)

